[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 10:38] That holds together. Beyond pricing, what else could the client do to reverse the decline?

[candidate · 12:08] A few levers.

Procurement: 200 locations is real scale — renegotiate supplier contracts, consolidate vendors, and hedge or forward-buy beans and dairy instead of buying spot.

Mix: push higher-margin items. Espresso drinks carry more margin than food or bulk brewed; adding a proprietary drink or subscription can raise ticket without a visible price hike.

Waste and portioning: if some of that COGS jump is spoilage or overpouring, tighter portion control recovers points with no customer impact.

Cheaper format on price: smaller sizes at the same price, or reducing cup sizes, which is effectively price without sticker shock.

But pricing is the main one. Inputs up ~38% and prices flat for two years means competitors almost certainly raised prices — do we have any competitor pricing data?
