[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 12:09] I don't have competitor pricing. Which lever leads, and why?

[candidate · 13:37] Pricing leads.

It's the only lever sized to the problem. COGS is up 16 points of revenue; procurement savings and waste reduction might recover two or three points realistically. Pricing can recover the full gap.

The math: to restore COGS to 42% of revenue at current input costs, revenue per unit needs to rise roughly 58/42, about 38%. That's too much at once — but a staged increase, say 10-12% now and another later, gets most of the way.

It's also fastest. A price change is immediate; supplier renegotiation takes a contract cycle.

Risk is volume loss. But inputs rose industry-wide, so competitors likely moved too, and specialty coffee buyers are relatively price-insensitive. I'd test in a few markets first.
